<Layout title="Why shared meaning matters" description="How context-led semantic modelling can make property information easier to understand, exchange, challenge and reuse without replacing every organisation's systems.">
  <h1>Why shared meaning matters</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Property information travels further than any one form or system. A description
    written for a listing may later inform a search, a valuation or a lending decision.
    The opportunity is to carry its meaning, context and evidence with it, so the next
    person can understand what they have received and judge whether it meets their need.
  </p>

  <h2 id="beyond-fields">Agree what the information means</h2>
  <p>
    Two systems can exchange a field called “value” perfectly and still misunderstand
    one another. Is it an asking price, an agreed purchase price, a professional valuation
    or an estimated rebuilding cost? Which property does it describe? At what date, for
    whose purpose, and on what evidence? A number and a currency cannot answer those
    questions on their own.
  </p>
  <p>
    Semantic modelling makes these distinctions explicit. It connects the amount to the
    kind of assessment, the subject, the date and the source. This gives people and
    software a shared basis for comparison. It also makes a mismatch visible before a
    value is reused for a purpose it was never intended to support.
  </p>

  <h2 id="local-precision">Keep the distinctions that professions need</h2>
  <p>
    A conveyancer, surveyor and lender look at the same transaction with different
    responsibilities. Their differences are often useful knowledge. A model should help
    them exchange information without requiring every profession to abandon its own
    language or reduce its work to somebody else's form.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our <a href="/semantic-modelling/bounded-contexts">context-led approach</a> gives
    each community responsibility for the meanings it understands. Where information
    crosses a boundary, the communities agree the connection explicitly. For example,
    a recorded inspection date and the date a report was issued may both matter, but
    they are not interchangeable simply because another system has a single “survey date”.
    Keeping both meanings can be more valuable than forcing a premature compromise.
  </p>

  <h2 id="less-reinterpretation">Reduce repeated interpretation</h2>
  <p>
    Every integration needs to understand the data it receives. With definitions tied
    only to individual applications, the next integration must rediscover why a field
    exists and what its codes mean. A published definition, stable reference and reviewed
    value set give suppliers something reusable to work from.
  </p>
  <p>
    Consider “not known”, “not applicable” and “no”. Treating them all as an empty field
    loses information. Agreeing their meanings once, with examples, can help forms,
    APIs and reports preserve the distinction. Integration still needs engineering and
    testing; the improvement is that teams can implement a visible agreement instead
    of separately guessing the business rules.
  </p>

  <h2 id="traceable-evidence">Make evidence easier to follow and challenge</h2>
  <p>
    Knowing a fact's source is part of understanding its usefulness. A statement from a
    seller, an observation by a surveyor and an entry in an authoritative register may
    describe related matters while offering different evidence. The model can keep the
    statement, its subject and its source distinct, together with relevant dates.
  </p>
  <p>
    This helps a reviewer ask precise questions: who supplied this information, what
    does it actually support, and what changed? It does not make every statement true.
    The benefit is an inspectable route back to the evidence and the people responsible
    for evaluating it, including when sources disagree.
  </p>

  <h2 id="change">Change one meaning coherently</h2>
  <p>
    An agreed definition is used in many places: a glossary entry, a data dictionary,
    a diagram, a set of permitted answers and a validation rule. Maintaining these as
    one <a href="/semantic-modelling/semantic-package">semantic package</a> makes it
    possible to identify which other descriptions need attention when a meaning changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The longer-term direction is to produce familiar schemas, forms and other delivery
    formats through tested transformations. That can reduce conflicting specifications,
    but each transformation must demonstrate what it preserves and what it leaves out.
    The <a href="/development/outputs">outputs register</a> distinguishes current
    artefacts from future capabilities; these benefits are not a claim that every
    generator is already available.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">Give AI a clearer account of the domain</h2>
  <p>
    Explicit meanings can also help AI systems distinguish a property from a title,
    an asking price from a valuation, and a person from a role they play. Definitions,
    relationships and source references give an application material it can retrieve
    and check rather than relying entirely on patterns in ordinary text.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is a foundation for better-grounded applications, not a promise of error-free
    answers. The same agreement remains useful to conventional software, search and
    human reviewers. Taking part does not require adopting AI or changing the technology
    used inside your organisation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="earn-benefits">Benefits have to be earned</h2>
  <p>
    A model earns its place when practitioners can recognise their work, integrations
    can preserve the distinctions they need, and changes can be explained. Useful
    measures include unanswered business questions, avoidable interpretation errors,
    human corrections and the effort required to implement a defined exchange. This
    section does not claim measured savings or guarantee faster transactions.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="/development/property-pack">Property Pack candidate</a> offers
    something concrete to inspect now. Its definitions are reviewable proposals, not
    settled answers for the entire sector. The strongest next step is practitioner
    challenge: <a href="/semantic-modelling/taking-part">see how to contribute</a>
    using examples and business language.
  </p>
</Layout>
